WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:07.05
This is journey twenty three of Hewn, the software factory: run workers locally or

00:00:07.05 --> 00:00:07.93
in the cloud.

00:00:07.93 --> 00:00:11.24
Workers are the processes that run Hewn's agents.

00:00:11.24 --> 00:00:16.92
This video shows one worker image running on a laptop with Docker and configured for

00:00:16.92 --> 00:00:21.25
the cloud, how an idle worker gets work the moment it is queued,

00:00:21.25 --> 00:00:23.62
and how each run is kept to itself.

00:00:24.77 --> 00:00:30.05
The same worker, configured as Local or as Cloud, registers with its location,

00:00:30.05 --> 00:00:33.98
host and version, and appears in Workers within 2 seconds.

00:00:33.98 --> 00:00:38.58
An idle worker leases new work at once and heartbeats while it runs.

00:00:38.58 --> 00:00:42.58
And every run is isolated: its own workspace and container,

00:00:42.58 --> 00:00:46.78
only allowed hosts on the network, and only its stage's tools,

00:00:46.78 --> 00:00:49.35
with anything else blocked and logged.

00:00:50.50 --> 00:00:52.27
Here is the worker image.

00:00:52.27 --> 00:00:58.21
It builds the worker from source, then copies it into a slim Node image with git and

00:00:58.21 --> 00:00:58.70
ffmpeg.

00:00:58.70 --> 00:01:02.24
It runs as an unprivileged user and opens no port,

00:01:02.24 --> 00:01:05.06
because a worker only ever connects out.

00:01:05.06 --> 00:01:09.59
Location, API address, token and name are environment variables,

00:01:09.59 --> 00:01:14.11
so they are the only differences between a laptop and the cloud.

00:01:14.46 --> 00:01:16.51
Here Workers is already open.

00:01:16.51 --> 00:01:22.38
One docker run starts the image as a Local worker, and it appears without a reload.

00:01:22.38 --> 00:01:25.98
A second starts the same image configured as Cloud.

00:01:26.33 --> 00:01:30.71
Each row shows the host, location and version the worker registered with.

00:01:31.86 --> 00:01:34.89
Workers used to refresh every five seconds.

00:01:34.89 --> 00:01:36.37
Now the API tells it.

00:01:36.37 --> 00:01:39.96
When a worker registers, or a stage starts or ends,

00:01:39.96 --> 00:01:44.40
the hub sends Workers Changed to everyone on the workers group.

00:01:44.75 --> 00:01:49.00
The shell subscribes through the live client and reads the list again.

00:01:49.00 --> 00:01:51.79
The five second refresh stays, for heartbeats.

00:01:52.94 --> 00:01:54.36
Here is the work loop.

00:01:54.36 --> 00:01:59.11
The worker registers, then asks for a lease with a wait of twenty seconds.

00:01:59.11 --> 00:02:02.52
If nothing is queued, the API holds the request open.

00:02:02.52 --> 00:02:07.47
Every stored event pulses a work signal, so the moment a direction is queued,

00:02:07.47 --> 00:02:09.20
the held request leases it.

00:02:09.20 --> 00:02:14.67
If nothing comes, the worker asks again fifteen seconds after its last request began,

00:02:14.67 --> 00:02:18.20
so an idle worker makes at most four requests a minute.

00:02:18.55 --> 00:02:22.24
Here a direction is submitted while both workers are idle.

00:02:22.24 --> 00:02:26.05
Its first stage starts within moments, not at the next poll.

00:02:26.05 --> 00:02:31.14
On Workers, the run shows as leased, and the heartbeat keeps refreshing while it

00:02:31.14 --> 00:02:31.46
runs.

00:02:32.61 --> 00:02:33.62
Now isolation.

00:02:33.62 --> 00:02:39.33
For every run, the work loop starts an egress proxy and hands each stage a tool

00:02:39.33 --> 00:02:39.69
gate.

00:02:40.04 --> 00:02:46.82
The proxy allows the model API, Magic Patterns and the npm and NuGet registries.

00:02:46.82 --> 00:02:53.51
GitHub isn't on the list: a run reaches its repository only through Hewn's API.

00:02:53.51 --> 00:02:58.17
Any other host gets a 403, and a line in the run's log.

00:02:58.52 --> 00:03:02.15
The tool gate is built from the stage's allowed tools.

00:03:02.15 --> 00:03:04.64
Every tool call goes through require.

00:03:04.64 --> 00:03:09.21
A tool that isn't allowed throws, and is logged as a security event.

00:03:09.56 --> 00:03:13.67
Commands the run executes get none of the worker's variables.

00:03:13.67 --> 00:03:17.97
With the docker sandbox, each command runs in its own container,

00:03:17.97 --> 00:03:21.27
with only the workspace mounted, no capabilities,

00:03:21.27 --> 00:03:24.84
and an internal network whose only peer is the proxy.

00:03:25.19 --> 00:03:30.48
Here is the whole run as a sequence: the proxy and the gate both write to the run's

00:03:30.48 --> 00:03:33.29
log, and nothing in the run talks to GitHub.

00:03:33.64 --> 00:03:37.39
Here a worker plays an agent misled by what it read.

00:03:37.39 --> 00:03:42.79
In the Idea stage, it tries the Bash tool and a request to an unknown host.

00:03:42.79 --> 00:03:47.47
Both are refused, and the log shows them, flagged error and warn.

00:03:47.82 --> 00:03:52.85
A d r twenty five records these choices, and why the git sidecar can wait.

00:03:54.00 --> 00:03:59.37
On the backend, a new test follows the hub and checks Workers is told within two

00:03:59.37 --> 00:04:02.32
seconds of a register, a lease and a finish.

00:04:02.32 --> 00:04:05.61
It failed first, as the hub had no workers group.

00:04:05.61 --> 00:04:09.64
The backend has two hundred and eighty two tests, all green.

00:04:09.99 --> 00:04:15.73
The worker's isolation tests run the proxy for real: an allowed host passes,

00:04:15.73 --> 00:04:20.10
others get a 403, and the run's log records both refusals.

00:04:20.10 --> 00:04:23.96
They failed first, with no isolation module at all.

00:04:23.96 --> 00:04:27.50
The worker has one hundred and fifty two tests.

00:04:27.85 --> 00:04:33.52
End to end, three new tests start real workers: Local and Cloud ones appearing in

00:04:33.52 --> 00:04:39.12
Workers, an idle worker pushed new work, and the refused tool and request in the

00:04:39.12 --> 00:04:39.82
run's log.

00:04:40.97 --> 00:04:44.21
Workers reads the same in dark mode, and on a phone.

00:04:45.36 --> 00:04:47.69
Journey twenty three is complete.

00:04:47.69 --> 00:04:51.50
One image runs anywhere, and configuration says where.

00:04:51.50 --> 00:04:55.45
New workers show at once, and idle ones are pushed work.

00:04:55.45 --> 00:05:00.95
Each run has its own proxy, gate and sandbox, and every refusal is in its log.

00:05:00.95 --> 00:05:03.13
Next comes journey twenty four.
